  "problems": [
    {
      "goal_step": 1,
      "title": "Definition of functions",
      "skill": "meaning",
      "question": [
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "Define what it means for a relation from a set \\(X\\) to a set \\(Y\\) to be a function."
        }
      ],
      "answer": [
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "A function from \\(X\\) to \\(Y\\) is a relation \\(f \\subseteq X \\times Y\\) such that for every \\(x \\in X\\), there is exactly one \\(y \\in Y\\) with \\((x,y) \\in f\\)\\(. \\)"
        }
      ],
      "source": {
        "document_id": "ffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffff",
        "page": 63,
        "line_start": null,
        "line_end": null,
        "quote": null
      }
    },
    {
      "goal_step": 2,
      "title": "Injectivity check for a function on integers",
      "skill": "application",
      "question": [
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "Let \\(f : \\mathbb{Z} \\to \\mathbb{Z}\\) be defined by \\(f(x) = 2x + 5.\\) Is \\(f\\) injective? Justify your answer."
        }
      ],
      "answer": [
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "Assume \\(f(x_1) = f(x_2)\\). Then, by definition, \\(2x_1 + 5 = 2x_2 + 5\\). Subtracting 5 from both sides gives \\(2x_1 = 2x_2\\). Dividing by 2, we get \\(x_1 = x_2\\). Hence, \\(f\\) is injective since equal outputs imply equal inputs."
        },
        {
          "kind": "math",
          "latex": "\\text{Source: Definition 12.8 and proof method from page 65}",
          "display": true,
          "source": {
            "document_id": "ffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffff",
            "page": 65,
            "line_start": null,
            "line_end": null,
            "quote": null
          },
          "original_latex": null,
          "change": null
        }
      ],
      "source": {
        "document_id": "ffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffff",
        "page": 65,
        "line_start": null,
        "line_end": null,
        "quote": null
      }
    },
    {
      "goal_step": 3,
      "title": "Surjectivity of a function on integers",
      "skill": "application",
      "question": [
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "Consider the function \\(f : \\mathbb{Z} \\to \\mathbb{Z}\\) defined by \\(f(m,n) = m - 2n\\), where the domain is \\(\\mathbb{Z} \\times \\mathbb{Z}\\) and the codomain is \\(\\mathbb{Z}\\). Determine whether \\(f\\) is surjective onto \\(\\mathbb{Z}\\)."
        }
      ],
      "answer": [
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "To show surjectivity, take arbitrary \\(z \\in \\mathbb{Z}\\) and find \\((m,n) \\in \\mathbb{Z}^2\\) so that \\(f(m,n) = z\\). Set \\(m = z + 2n\\) for any integer \\(n\\). Then \\(f(m,n) = m - 2n = (z + 2n) - 2n = z\\). Since this works for any \\(z\\) and some \\((m,n)\\), \\(f\\) is surjective onto \\(\\mathbb{Z}\\)."
        },
        {
          "kind": "math",
          "latex": "\\text{Source: Surjectivity method page 65}",
          "display": true,
          "source": {
            "document_id": "ffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffff",
            "page": 65,
            "line_start": null,
            "line_end": null,
            "quote": null
          },
          "original_latex": null,
          "change": null
        }
      ],
      "source": {
        "document_id": "ffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffff",
        "page": 65,
        "line_start": null,
        "line_end": null,
        "quote": null
      }
    },
    {
      "goal_step": 4,
      "title": "Bijectivity and inverse construction for function on integers pairs",
      "skill": "why_justification",
      "question": [
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "Let \\(f : \\mathbb{Z} \\times \\mathbb{Z} \\to \\mathbb{Z} \\times \\mathbb{Z}\\) be given by \\(f(m,n) = (2m + n, m + 3n)\\). Prove that \\(f\\) is bijective and construct its inverse function."
        }
      ],
      "answer": [
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "To prove injectivity, assume \\(f(m,n) = f(a,b)\\) meaning \\((2m + n, m + 3n) = (2a + b, a + 3b)\\). This gives the system:"
        },
        {
          "kind": "math",
          "latex": "\\begin{cases} 2m + n = 2a + b \\\\ m + 3n = a + 3b \\end{cases}",
          "display": true,
          "source": {
            "document_id": "ffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffff",
            "page": 65,
            "line_start": null,
            "line_end": null,
            "quote": null
          },
          "original_latex": null,
          "change": null
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "Subtract the first equation multiplied by 3 from the second multiplied by 1:"
        },
        {
          "kind": "math",
          "latex": "(m + 3n) - 3(2m + n) = (a + 3b) - 3(2a + b)",
          "display": true,
          "source": {
            "document_id": "ffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffff",
            "page": 65,
            "line_start": null,
            "line_end": null,
            "quote": null
          },
          "original_latex": null,
          "change": null
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "This simplifies to \\(m + 3n - 6m - 3n = a + 3b - 6a - 3b\\) or \\(-5m = -5a\\) implying \\(m = a\\). Substitute \\(m = a\\) into first equation to get \\(2m + n = 2m + b\\) hence \\(n = b\\). Thus \\(f\\) is injective."
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "To prove surjectivity, for any \\((x,y) \\in \\mathbb{Z} \\times \\mathbb{Z}\\) solve:"
        },
        {
          "kind": "math",
          "latex": "\\begin{cases} 2m + n = x \\\\ m + 3n = y \\end{cases}",
          "display": true,
          "source": {
            "document_id": "ffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffff",
            "page": 65,
            "line_start": null,
            "line_end": null,
            "quote": null
          },
          "original_latex": null,
          "change": null
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "Rewrite as linear system:"
        },
        {
          "kind": "math",
          "latex": "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\begin{bmatrix}m \\\\ n\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}",
          "display": true,
          "source": {
            "document_id": "ffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffff",
            "page": 65,
            "line_start": null,
            "line_end": null,
            "quote": null
          },
          "original_latex": null,
          "change": null
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "The determinant is \\(2 \\cdot 3 - 1 \\cdot 1 = 5 \\neq 0\\) so invertible. The inverse matrix is "
        },
        {
          "kind": "math",
          "latex": "\\frac{1}{5} \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}",
          "display": true,
          "source": {
            "document_id": "ffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffff",
            "page": 65,
            "line_start": null,
            "line_end": null,
            "quote": null
          },
          "original_latex": null,
          "change": null
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "Thus:"
        },
        {
          "kind": "math",
          "latex": "\\begin{bmatrix}m \\\\ n\\end{bmatrix} = \\frac{1}{5} \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} = \\left(\\frac{3x - y}{5}, \\frac{-x + 2y}{5} \\right)",
          "display": true,
          "source": {
            "document_id": "ffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffff",
            "page": 65,
            "line_start": null,
            "line_end": null,
            "quote": null
          },
          "original_latex": null,
          "change": null
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "Since \\(m, n\\) must be integers, for \\(f\\) to be surjective over integers, \\((3x - y)\\) and \\((-x + 2y)\\) must be divisible by 5. Hence, \\(f\\) is not surjective onto entire \\(\\mathbb{Z} \\times \\mathbb{Z}\\) but onto the subset where this divisibility holds."
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "Since \\(f\\) is injective but not surjective onto all of \\(\\mathbb{Z} \\times \\mathbb{Z}\\), it is not bijective over this codomain. Restricting the codomain to the image makes it bijective, with inverse given by above formula."
        },
        {
          "kind": "math",
          "latex": "\\text{Source: Injectivity and surjectivity methods on page 65}",
          "display": true,
          "source": {
            "document_id": "ffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffff",
            "page": 65,
            "line_start": null,
            "line_end": null,
            "quote": null
          },
          "original_latex": null,
          "change": null
        }
      ],
      "source": {
        "document_id": "ffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffff",
        "page": 65,
        "line_start": null,
        "line_end": null,
        "quote": null
      }
    }
